WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.103
Data access is part of REST API security
because handlers often stand between

2
00:00:04.183 --> 00:00:06.567
untrusted requests and durable business
records.

3
00:00:06.647 --> 00:00:10.305
Go code should use safe database access
patterns, parameterized operations,

4
00:00:10.385 --> 00:00:13.878
clear repository or service contracts, and
reviewed query construction.

5
00:00:13.958 --> 00:00:17.395
The goal is that untrusted input does not
become executable behavior or an

6
00:00:17.475 --> 00:00:18.639
uncontrolled selector.

7
00:00:18.719 --> 00:00:21.546
Validation should happen before storage or
downstream use.

8
00:00:21.626 --> 00:00:25.395
A decoded request should not be written
directly to the database just because its

9
00:00:25.475 --> 00:00:26.418
fields fit a struct.

10
00:00:26.498 --> 00:00:29.657
The service should check shape, meaning,
ownership, tenant scope,

11
00:00:29.737 --> 00:00:33.230
and workflow rules before it creates,
updates, deletes, or forwards data.

12
00:00:33.310 --> 00:00:35.693
Output safety is just as important as
input safety.

13
00:00:35.773 --> 00:00:39.765
APIs should return explicit response
models rather than automatically serializing

14
00:00:39.845 --> 00:00:40.731
internal records.

15
00:00:40.811 --> 00:00:44.082
Internal database models may contain
fields that clients do not need,

16
00:00:44.162 --> 00:00:48.042
such as administrative notes, internal
flags, authorization metadata, secrets,

17
00:00:48.122 --> 00:00:50.617
or fields that become sensitive as the
model evolves.

18
00:00:50.697 --> 00:00:53.081
Response shaping helps preserve least
privilege.

19
00:00:53.161 --> 00:00:56.930
It also gives teams a clear place to
review which fields are safe for which caller.

20
00:00:57.010 --> 00:00:59.837
Downstream service data should receive the
same treatment.

21
00:00:59.917 --> 00:01:03.797
A response from another system should be
validated and minimized before it becomes

22
00:01:03.877 --> 00:01:05.042
part of the API response.

23
00:01:05.122 --> 00:01:08.725
Tenant separation must be preserved in
data access, not only in route checks.

24
00:01:08.805 --> 00:01:12.574
Queries, repository methods, cache keys,
object references, file references,

25
00:01:12.654 --> 00:01:16.313
and pagination should carry the tenant or
ownership context needed to prevent

26
00:01:16.393 --> 00:01:18.278
accidental cross-scope data exposure.

27
00:01:18.358 --> 00:01:21.185
Safe APIs control both what they accept
and what they return.

28
00:01:21.265 --> 00:01:23.759
Transaction boundaries should also be
intentional.

29
00:01:23.839 --> 00:01:26.888
When a route changes multiple records or
calls multiple systems,

30
00:01:26.968 --> 00:01:29.241
failure handling should preserve a clear
state.

31
00:01:29.321 --> 00:01:31.926
Data access code should make partial
updates, retries,

32
00:01:32.006 --> 00:01:33.780
and cleanup visible enough to review.
